ystem and is essential for performing arithmetic operations accurately.\n</think>.\n<answer>. In the decimal system, we use base 10, which means we have 10 different digits (0 through 9) to represent any number. When we add numbers, we align them by place value, starting from the rightmost digit (units place) and moving left. \n\nWhen the sum of digits in a place value exceeds 9, we encounter a situation where we have more than one digit to represent the value. For example, if we add 8 and 6, we get 14. In the decimal system, we cannot have a value of 14 in the units place, so we write down the 4 and carry over the 1 to the next place value. This is because the 1 represents 10 units, which we add to the next place value.\n\nThis process of carrying over is a fundamental aspect of the decimal system and is necessary to correctly represent numbers in this base. It ensures that we are always working with valid digits (0 through 9) in each place value, and it allows us to handle larger numbers by breaking them down into smaller, manageable parts.\n\nIn the context of the problem, when we add 820318 and 602086, we carry over when the sum of digits in any place value exceeds 9. This is why we had to carry over in the tens place from the units place, and again in the ten-thousands place from the thousands place. Each carry-over represents a shift in the value of the digits, moving them to the next higher place value.\n\nUnderstanding this process helps in grasping the mechanics of addition in the decimal system and is essential for performing arithmetic operations accurately.</answer>",
